1450:
(a) All users involved in the preparation of food for commercial purposes shall provide approved fat, oil, grease (FOG) removal equipment such as separators or traps, if:
(1) The user generates a washwater which contains greater than the quantity of FOG permitted in these regulations; and/or
(2) Allows commercial waste that emits noxious or offensive odors or is unsanitary or injurious to public health or property to spill, overflow, or be discharged onto public or private property;
(3) Allows commercial waste to be removed from a facility under his/her control except by a transporter holding a valid and current license/permit for pumping in the state;
(4) Allows commercial waste generated at a facility under his/her control to be disposed of at a site not permitted by the city;
(5) Allows commercial waste generated at a facility under his/her control to enter the city sewerage system which exceeds the maximum limitations set forth herein.
(Ord. No. 2019-06, § 3, 10-15-19)

1457:
(a) A commercial waste generator shall not allow a transporter to remove waste from its facility without a current commercial waste hauler permit issued by the state or local governing authority. In addition, a generator:
(1) Shall not permit a transporter to remove commercial waste from the generator's premises until the transporter has presented a non-hazardous manifest to the generator to document pumping activity. This manifest will require signatures from the generator, transporter, and disposer in order to maintain and establish accountability. The manifest should be a multi-copy form;
(2) Shall ensure that the manifest form used to document the transportation and disposal of commercial waste generated in the city contains the following information at a minimum:
a. Generator information:
1. Name and physical address of facility;
2. Volume of waste pumped;
3. Date and time of pumping activity;
4. Signature of authorized representative; and
5. Print name.
b. Transporter information:
1. Name and physical address of company;
2. County or issued commercial waste FOG permit;
3. Driver certification number;
4. Volume of waste pumped;
5. Name and signature of driver; and
(6. Print name.
c. Disposer information:
1. Name and physical address of facility;
2. State, county, city, or federal permit number of site;
3. Volume of waste received;
4. Signature of authorized representative; and
5. Print name.
(3) Upon receipt of the completed copy of the manifest from the transporter, shall mail a copy of the manifest within five (5) days to the following:
Office of Code Enforcement 5047 Union Street Union City, Georgia 30291;
(4) Shall accurately complete and sign the generator information section of the manifest to ensure that the transporter has noted on the manifest the number of gallons of waste removed and verify that the generator information is correct;
(5) Shall keep a copy of all manifests for a period of three (3) years on site and shall make said copies available for inspection by the city; and
(6) Shall report any spills to the city upon becoming aware of a spill that could impact any surrounding areas such as storm drains, adjacent streams, or the ground surface where the transporter has removed waste from the facility's pretreatment system. Failure to notify the city of a spill will constitute a violation and fines may be assessed to the commercial waste generator as well as the transporter.
(Ord. No. 2019-06, § 10, 10-15-19)

1458:
An annual permit will be required for all pretreatment systems, both existing and new. A fee will be charged for this permit. The permit fee will be based on the type of pretreatment system at the site. Failure to apply for a commercial waste permit shall be a violation. The generator's food service permit may be suspended until the permit is obtained.
(a) All commercial waste permits shall be issued annually from January 1 to March 31. Any existing facility obtaining a permit after March 31 will be charged a penalty fee as set out in a schedule of fees and charges adopted by the mayor and council. A new facility opening after the annual permit period will not pay a penalty unless it has been determined that the facility has been opened for more than thirty (30) days. After the facility has been opened for thirty (30) days, the penalty fee shall apply. A new facility applying for a commercial waste permit after the permitting period will not pay a prorated fee. The cost of an annual permit shall be assessed according to a schedule of fees and charges adopted by the mayor and council.
(b) Permit conditions shall include, but are not limited to the following:
(1) Permit duration;
(2) Permit fee;
(3) Permit transfer;
(4) Frequency of inspections;
(5) Pretreatment requirements;
(6) Maintenance requirements;
(7) Compliance schedules;
(8) Requirements for maintenance records and submission of reports;
(9) Right of entry;
(10) Review of upgrades or repairs of a pretreatment system; and
(11) Other conditions deemed necessary to ensure compliance with this article or other applicable ordinances, laws, or regulations.
(Ord. No. 2019-06, § 11, 10-15-19; Ord. No. 2024-01, § 6, 1-23-24)

1459:
(a) Wherever the use of a subsurface pretreatment system is discontinued due to a business closing or relocation, and the system is connected to the sanitary sewer, the commercial waste generator shall implement the following procedure:
(1) Contact the commercial pretreatment inspection group before vacating the property;
(2) Resolve all outstanding violations; and
(3) Clean the pretreatment system before vacating the business and submit a manifest to the city to substantiate the cleaning of the system.
(b) If the commercial waste generator fails to clean the pretreatment system and/or resolve outstanding violations it shall be considered a violation. Management or property leasing will be notified. No commercial waste generator shall be allowed to occupy the facility until it is brought into compliance. If the existing pretreatment system is not adequate based on the new business operation, then the new commercial waste generator shall upgrade the pretreatment system. If the building or tenant space vacated will not be used for another commercial waste generator, then management leasing or the building owner shall implement the following tank abandonment procedures:
(1) Collapse tank or fill pretreatment system with sand;
(2) Seal over manholes so that they are no longer accessible;
(3) Disconnect or reroute the kitchen waste line from the building to the pretreatment system; and
(4) Contact the city to inspect the abandoned system prior to a new business opening.
(Ord. No. 2019-06, § 12, 10-15-19)

1461:
The following will constitute a construction violation:
(a) Constructing, altering, modifying, repairing, or installing a pretreatment system without receiving a permit from the city;
(b) Installing, modifying, or repairing a pretreatment system which will violate standards set forth herein, without evidence that a variance has been granted;
(c) Performing services which will cause or increase the likelihood of pollution to the sanitary sewer, storm drains, or publically-owned treatment works and/or pose a threat to the health and safety of the public;
(d) Failing to maintain records of services and/or providing a copy of those records to the city upon request. An example of this requirement would be an invoice verifying the purchase of the pretreatment system;
(e) Initiating work to install, upgrade, or repair a system where no permit has been issued by the city. It shall also be a violation of sections 15-215 to 15-230 if a permit is issued after construction is started but prior to completion of the contracted work or if no site inspections are performed;
(f) Completing contracted work without a permit having been issued or if no permit application was received until contracted work was completed, resulting in missed inspections;
(g) Failing to call for required inspections;
(h) Abandoning, without good cause, a project in which a contractor is engaged or is under contractual obligation to perform. The failure of a contractor to perform work without just cause for ninety (90) consecutive days shall create a presumption that the contractor has abandoned the project;
(i) Gross negligence, incompetence, or misconduct which causes monetary or other harm to a customer or physical harm to any person, e.g., the illegal disposal of a generator's waste when repairing a pretreatment system; and
(j) Installing a pretreatment system by someone other than a licensed contractor or master plumber.
(Ord. No. 2019-06, § 14, 10-15-19)

1463:
When the designated city representative finds that a user has violated, or continues to violate, any provision herein, a commercial waste permit or any order issued hereunder, or any other pretreatment standard or requirement, the designated city representative may issue a citation to such user for each violation. Each day that a violation continues shall constitute a separate offense and shall be punished accordingly.
(1) Issuance of a citation shall not be a bar against, or a prerequisite for, taking any other action.
(2) The following shall be considered violations herein and the responsible person or company may be assessed fines accordingly:
a. Having a licensed contractor, engineer, or plumber install, modify, or repair a pretreatment system without a pretreatment permit or to work on the installation of a pretreatment system and obtain a permit alter work has commenced. It shall also be a violation if no inspections are performed;
b. Installation of a pretreatment system by a commercial waste generator without submitting plans for review. It shall also be a violation if installation of a system is completed and no permit application is received or on file and if no final inspections of the project are performed;
c. Failure to call for required inspection(s) when a renovation or upgrade of a pretreatment system is required;
d. A commercial waste generator's failure to maintain its pretreatment system;
e. A commercial waste generator's failure to provide the city with a manifest or maintenance documentation for its pretreatment system:
f. A commercial waste generator's failure to inform the city that a spill has occurred during pumping of a pretreatment system and the spill impacts the sanitary sewer, storm drain, or environment; and
g. A commercial waste generator's failure to apply for the required commercial waste permit for a pretreatment system in the city.
(Ord. No. 2019-06, § 16, 10-15-19)
